{"kind":"artifact","text":"Answers changes-requested verdict a9564eb5 on df387c9a. The fold reads a retirement for what its own act rested on. A supersession resting on an artifact covering the same path - the same string, or a directory containing it - is succession: Artifact.Succeeded is set and that retirement carries no staleness across reasoning edges, so the approval, report, promise, request and commitment of a completed loop do not flare on the merge that completed them. A bare supersession is condemnation and propagates as before. Succession now carries the successor link rather than a boolean, and staleness follows the chain to its end: a successor later retired with no successor of its own condemns the behaviour, and everything that stood on the predecessor - approval, requests, promises, commitments, and the merge receipt, whose signed plan stops covering a condemned chain - flares then, exactly as if its own basis had been withdrawn. A successor replaced by a further successor keeps answering. Artifact-to-artifact provenance carries the flare either way, so documentation above a replaced implementation still goes stale and reads describes_superseded_world. ProfileVersion workroom-fold@6. gs status --all renders SUCCEEDED apart from RETIRED. Tests in internal/workroom/fold_test.go: TestSuccessionLeavesTheCompletedLoopUnflared, TestRetirementWithoutASuccessorStillFlaresTheLoop, TestLaterArtifactAtTheSamePathDoesNotRescueACondemnedOne, TestSuccessionStillFlaresTheDocumentationAboveIt, TestSuccessionFollowsOnlyACoveringSuccessor, TestRefusedSupersessionRecordsNoSuccession, and the new TestCondemnedSuccessorFlaresWhatStoodOnThePredecessor covering condemned, replaced, and condemned-at-the-end-of-the-chain. Mutation-checked at this head: making successionCondemned always false fails the new test; earlier mutations of the succession rule still fail their tests. Gates at this exact head: gofmt, git diff --check and go vet clean; go test -count=1 ./... green in 